## 1. Introduction
With the rapid development of electronic communication equipment, electromagnetic (EM) pollution and interference are becoming more and more serious, and have caused great harm to electronic equipment, human health and the ecological environment, etc [1–4]. Developing EM wave (EMW) absorption materials (EMAs) can help to address these issues, and EMAs have a wide range of applications including building materials, radar stealth, electronic components and electronic equipment [5–8]. Current research has shown that the high performance of EMAs is dependent on their structure. It has been confirmed by decades of research that three-dimensional nano EMAs especially core–shell EMAs have huge convenience due to their stronger absorption strength, wider absorption frequency band and thinner absorption layer thickness [9–12]. The current research mainly focused on the material optimization combination design, the coupling behavior between core–shell interface, and the core–shell space construction. However, there are few studies on the phase inversion adjustment of core–shell structures to optimize their EMW performance.
In general, obtaining simultaneous and even synergistic effects between cores and shells is the prime objective of constructing core–shell materials [13]. Tremendous works have shown that core–shell structures can not only alleviate the impedance mismatch but also have the ability to attenuate EMW by tailoring the heterogeneous interfaces and reflection channels [14]. Ordinarily, for dielectric materials, their dielectric loss is usually optimized by regulating their polarization loss and conductive loss [15]. On one hand, enhancing the polarization loss of EMAs including dipoles polarization and interfacial polarization can obviously ameliorate their ability to attenuate EMW [16]. For instance, Wen et al. prepared core–shell $\mathsf{C o@S}$ -doped carbon $@$ mesoporous $N\cdot$ -doped carbon nanosheets to enhance their polarization loss, resulting in their outstanding absorption performance [17]. On the other hand, improving the materials’ conductivity is beneficial for their conductive loss and impedance matching. Jia et al. constructed $\scriptstyle\mathrm{MnO}_{2}@$ polyaniline core–shell heterojunction with enhanced conductive loss, which also exhibited excellent absorption performance [18]. By and large, the manipulation of polarization loss and conductive loss by regulating core–shell structures helps to optimize the impedance matching and EMW attenuation. This can increase the absorption of EMW effectively [19]. In this case, the core–shell structure has broad application prospects for optimizing EMW absorption.
At present, various strategies have been devoted to preparing EMAs with core–shell structure, including sol–gel method [20], hydrothermal method [21], in-situ polymerization method [22], electroless plating method and arc method [23], etc. The microscopic morphology of the as-prepared products did not only have spherical shapes but also one-dimensional nanomaterials (such as nanowire, nanorod, nanofiber and nanotube), two-dimensional sheets [24], cube shape and other irregular shapes [25]. Metalorganic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted much attention as precursors of EMAs due to their periodic network structure, large specific surface area and tunable chemical structures [26]. The derived products can have satisfactory EMW absorption performance by adjusting their metal ions and organic ligands. The rational regulation of MOFs to obtain EMAs with core–shell structure can be an extremely effective strategy, in which complicated interactions may be effective sources for polarization loss and conductive loss [27]. Generally, ‘‘top-down” and ‘‘bottom-up” are the mainstreams for fabricating MOFs derived core–shell structured EMAs [28–30], while the ligand exchange-guided phase inversion MOFs to prepare excellent EMAs has not been studied in detail.
In this work, a novel phase inversion strategy has been proposed to prepare core–shell $\mathsf{A g@C}$ spheres on Ag-MOF-5 by controllable ligand exchange. That is, a continuous amount of terephthalic acid $\mathrm{\langleH_{2}B D C\rangle}$ was applied to rearrange the coordination structure among ${\sf A g}^{+}$ , $\mathtt{H}_{2}\mathtt{B D C}$ and 2-methylimidazole (Hmim) in Ag-MOF-5. After the precursors were calcinated properly core– shell $\mathsf{A g@C}$ spheres with tunable crystal and morphological structures were obtained, which determined their electromagnetic response and EMW absorption performance. Especially, when the content of Hmim was twice that of Ag-MOF-5, the optimal minimal reflection loss $(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min.}})$ reached $-50.14~\mathrm{dB}$ at $3\ \mathrm{mm}$ . The product derived from pure Ag-MOF-5 had the broadest effective absorption band $(f_{\mathrm{E}},$ $\mathrm{RL}<10\$ dB) of $5.44\:\mathrm{GHz}$ with a thickness of only $2.2~\mathrm{mm}$ . The product had such excellent performances due to the core–shell hierarchical structure and improved conductivity, leading to satisfied impedance matching and enhanced EMW attenuation. In addition, since the fabricated sample showed no magnetic loss, the single mechanism of dielectric loss can be investigated systematically. This work will provide theoretical guidance for the design of phase-inversion EMAs, and it is helpful to deeply understand and explore the influence of single mechanism of dielectric loss on EMW absorption performance.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
2-methylimidazole (Hmim) (AR), terephthalic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ (AR) and silver nitrate $\left({\sf A g N O}_{3}\right)$ (AR) were provided by Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) (AR) was purchased from Guangdong Guanghua Sci-Tech Co., Ltd. Deionized water was made by a water purification device at the laboratory.
## 2.2. Preparation of Ag@C
The ligand exchange technology was employed to prepare phase-inversion type MOFs precursors for $\mathsf{A g@C}$ spheres. Firstly, $1.9\mathrm{~g~H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ and $5.74\mathrm{~g~AgNO}_{3}$ were reacted at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $160~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The desired product, Ag-MOF-5 was obtained after filtration and drying. Secondly, 2.0 mmol Ag-MOF-5 was dispersed in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water to form suspension A, and $2.0\ \mathrm{mmol}$ Hmim was also dissolved in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water to form solution B. Then, solution B was poured into suspension A quickly. The mixture was reacted at room temperature for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ under stirring. Subsequently, 1:1 MOF precursor was obtained after separation by centrifugation. The precursor was dried in a vacuum oven at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After calcinating the above precursor at 700 $\mathcal{\Bar{C}}$ for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ in Ar atmosphere, ${\mathsf{A g@C}}$ (S1) was obtained. Analogously, S0, S2, S3 and S4 can be obtained by the same process with different contents of Hmim added; that is, the mole ratios of Hmin to AgMOF-5 of S0, S2, S3, S4 are 0, 2, 3, 4, respectively.
## 2.3. Characterization
X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis was performed on an Ultima IV X-ray diffractometer under the radiation of $\operatorname{Cu}\operatorname{k}\alpha$ $\uplambda=0.15418\mathrm{nm}$ ) from $5^{\circ}<2\uptheta<90^{\circ}$ at a scanning rate of $2^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}$ . PHI 5000 VersaProbe III X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was used to characterize the chemical states of the corresponding samples. The microscopic morphology analysis was studied with a field-emission scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Verios G4, FEI, America), which was equipped with an energy dispersive X-ray (EDS) accessory to observe the distribution of elements. A Plus JEOL JEM-2100 transmission electron microscope (TEM) equipped with an energy dispersive X-ray (EDS) accessory was employed to observe the morphological properties of $\mathsf{A g@C}$ core–shell structures.
The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was tested with a CHI 660E electrochemical workstation from 0.1 to $100000\mathrm{Hz}$ , which was at a potential perturbation of open-circuit voltage in a typical three-electrode system. For detection, the nickel foam electrode was prepared by mixing Ag-ZIF, polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF), and Super $\mathrm{~\sf~P~}$ carbon black in Nmethylpyrrolidone with a mass ratio of 8:1:1. Then, it was applied on the nickel foam. ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt S O}_{4}$ aqueous solution (1 M) was used as an electrolyte, while a saturated calomel electrode (SCE) and a Pt foil were utilized as reference and counter electrodes, respectively.
All the wave absorbents were mixed into paraffin wax-based cylinder devices $\cdot\Phi_{\mathrm{out}}=7.00~\mathrm{mm}$ , $\phi_{\mathrm{in}}=3.04~\mathrm{mm}$ ) to acquire the complex permittivity $\begin{array}{r c l}{{\displaystyle\left(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}}}&{{=}}&{{\varepsilon^{\prime}{-}j\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\right)}}\end{array}$ and complex permeability $(\mu_{\mathrm{r}}=\mu^{\prime}\ –j\mu^{\prime\prime})$ parameters in the frequency range between 2 and 18 GHz with an Agilent N5234A vector network analyzer (VNA).
## 3. Result and discussion
## 3.1. Phase inversion strategy toward Ag-MOFs for Ag@C EMAs
The process of preparing Ag-ZIF by ligand exchange-guided phase inversion method is illustrated in Scheme 1. It is worth mentioning that both $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ and Hmim can coordinate with ${\sf A g}^{+}$ , but Hmim coordinates very strongly with ${\sf A g}^{+}$ in aqueous solution, according to hard/soft/acid/base and crystal field theory [31], which is an important prerequisite for ligand exchange and phase inversion. In detail, in the solution system of ligand exchangeguided phase inversion, $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ was in-situ replaced by Hmim to generate new Ag-N coordination bonds on the defects and edges of Ag-MOF-5. On one hand, Ag-O interactions in the original MOF-s were declined and even destroyed to release linkerdeficient ${\sf A g}^{+}$ for Hmim. On the other hand, Hmim could etch the MOFs to be a 3D solid spherical structure with cladding decoration. The XRD (Scheme 1) patterns and FTIR (Fig. S1) spectra showed the process of phase inversion. It can be observed that the diffraction peaks of MOF-5 structure were weakened and the diffraction peaks of ZIF-L structure appeared gradually [32]. The weakened stretching vibration peak of –COOH at $3075\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and the increasing characteristic peaks of –NH- for Hmim at $3120\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ also confirmed the successful exchange of the ligands. All in all, the controllable ligand exchange-guided phase inversion was further confirmed by the eclipsing XRD intensity, roughening morphology and increasing N elements signals in EDS elements maps and spectra. The AgMOFs prepared by phase inversion were pyrolyzed at high a temperature to form $\mathsf{A g@C}$ core–shell spheres, which assembled into larger spheres.
## 3.2. Structure of Ag based MOFs for Ag@C
The crystal and morphological structures of $\mathsf{A g@C}$ were characterized by the XRD patterns and SEM images (Fig. 1 and Fig. 2). Obviously, the Ag phase had a typical cubic symmetry with Fm$3\ m$ space group $(\mathsf{A g}\#\ 04\ –0783)$ (Fig. 1(a)) [33]. The diffraction peaks at $38.08^{\circ}$ , $44.32^{\circ}$ , $64.44^{\circ}$ and $77.35^{\circ}$ can be ascribed to (111), (200), (220) and (311) of cubic Ag, respectively. Due to the proper ratio of ligand exchanging phase inversion, S2 sample displays a better diffraction peak on the crystal lattice of cubic Ag, which has a better conduction loss compared to the other samples. The particle size of $\mathsf{A g@C}$ has a tendency of increasing first and then decreasing (Fig. 1(b)) because the supporting effect of the carbon skeleton reduces the particle size [34]. Specifically, the ligand exchange of Hmim at the edge and defect destroyed the carbon skeleton of the original MOFs, which made the particle size to increase initially. When the coordination exchange of Hmim reached a certain level, the carbon skeleton of the fresh MOFs was formed to reduce the particle size. This might be because the newly formed carbon skeleton was severely damaged. The SEM technology was used to study the morphological characteristics of the crystal structure and provide supporting evidence of the structure in Fig. 2(a, b) and Fig. S2. For samples S0 and S1, it can be observed that the $\mathsf{A g@C}$ spheres are 56.17 $(\pm26.18)$ and 56.37 $(\pm15.80)$ nm, respectively. In Fig. 1(b), the particle diameters of $\mathsf{A g}$ unit cells in samples S0 and S1 are 23.94 and $36.92\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. Moreover, compared to the other samples, S2 has the largest particle diameter of $\mathsf{A g}$ unit cell, which is consistent with the XRD analysis. These facts fully demonstrate that the as-prepared absorbers have core–shell structures. All the pyrolyzed products inherited the microstructures of the prepared precursor, Ag-MOFs, in which the coating process was carried out along the surface of the Ag unit to form a sphere with a core–shell structure. For S0 and S1, the distribution of $\mathsf{A g@C}$ particle size did not change significantly. This indicates that during the initial ligand exchange process, the carbon skeletons of the original MOFs were not obviously damaged; thus, the particle size of the formed core–shell $\mathsf{A g@C}$ did not increase significantly.
The whole region of XPS spectrum (Fig. S3) has distinctive C1s, N1s, O1s and $\mathtt{A g3d}$ characteristic peaks due to the successful synthesis of $\mathsf{A g@C}$ . More importantly, the core-level XPS fitting curves were used for the quantitative analysis of atomic surface concentration. Fig. 1(c-f) and Fig. S4 show the high-resolution peaks of Ag3d, N1s, C1s and O1s. Two major $\mathtt{A g3d}$ doublets are shown in Fig. 1(c); they are located at 374.1 eV $(\mathsf{A g3d}_{3/2})$ and $368.1\ \mathrm{~eV}$ $(\mathsf{A g3d}_{5/2})$ , respectively. The neighboring peaks at higher binding energy are attributed to their satellite peaks correspondingly. What’s more, $\mathsf{A g}^{0}$ and $\mathsf{A g}^{+}\mathsf{A g}3\mathsf{d}_{3/2}$ and $\mathsf{A g3d}_{5/2}$ spectra are located at 374.2, 373.7, 368.4 and $367.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively [35]. It can be observed that the content of ${\mathsf{A}}{\mathsf{g}}^{+}$ first increases and then decreases with the increase of Hmim content. This indicates that the destruction of the carbon skeleton of the original MOFs contributed to the production of ${\mathsf{A}}{\mathsf{g}}^{+}$ . When Hmim added was twice that of Ag-MOF-5, the content of the generated ${\sf A g}^{+}$ became the highest. Secondly, the residual N fraction from Ag-ZIF is detected by N1s spectroscopy (Fig. 1(d)), and three peaks at 398.1, 399.6, and $402.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be synthesized, which are assigned to imino group pyridinic $(=N-)$ , pyrrolic (–NH-) and graphitic $\begin{array}{r}{\left(\mathbb{R}\mathrm{-}\mathbb{N}\mathbb{H}_{2}\right)}\end{array}$ , respectively [36]. As the degree of ligand exchange increased, Hmim gradually replaced $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ to coordinate with ${\sf A g}^{+}$ , making the $\mathsf{A g-O}$ bond to decrease and the Ag-N bond to increase. With the enhanced Ag-N coordination and increased Hmim, the N modification of the carbon matrices of $\mathsf{A g@C}$ increased correspondingly, making the N XPS signals more and more obvious. The high-resolution C1s spectrum (Fig. 1 (e) and Fig. S4) can be mainly decomposed into four peaks, which are concentrated at 284.6, 285.4, 286.7, and $288.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ ; they correspond to $\mathtt{C-C/C=C,C-N,}N\mathtt{-C=N}$ and $0{\cdot}C{=}0$ , respectively [37]. It can be seen that the ratio of $0{\cdot}C{=}0$ decreases with increased Hmim content, and the increase in the ratio of $N-C=N$ and $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ indicates that $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{D B C}$ is gradually replaced by Hmim. Generally, the O species (Fig. 1(f) and Fig. S4) can be divided into four subpeaks located at 530.5, 530.8, 531.9 and $533.4~\mathrm{eV}$ , relating to $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ , ${\mathrm{-OH}}/{\mathrm{0}}_{2}$ , oxygen vacancies $\phantom{+}0\phantom{+}\phantom{.}$ oxygen lattice $0_{\mathrm{L}}$ [38]. It can be seen that the $0\mathrm{v}$ and $0_{\mathrm{L}}$ increase first and then decrease as the content of Hmim increases, which may lead to the strongest dipoles polarization of S2 [39].
The core–shell structure of $\mathsf{A g@C}$ spheres was further determined by TEM. It is obviously observed in Fig. 2(c-f) and Fig. S5 that S2 has core–shell structure. The unique core–shell structure consisted of carbon shell and the Ag unit cell core helped to attenuate EMW [40]. The EDS element mapping of $\mathsf{A g@C}$ (Fig. 2(e)) shows the distribution of C, N, O, and Ag. The distribution of Ag, N and O illustrates that the Ag particles coated by carbon spheres were successfully prepared. Furthermore, the high-resolution TEM image and SAED image in Fig. 2(f) determined the typical cubic crystalline structure of $\mathsf{A g}$ particles, which is in good accordance with XRD analysis [41].
## 3.3. Electromagnetic and EMW absorption properties
$$
\varepsilon^{'}=\varepsilon_{\infty}+\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{\left(2\pi f\right)^{2}\tau^{2}+1}
$$
$$
\varepsilon^{'}=\dot{\varepsilon_{p}}+\ddot{\varepsilon_{c}}=\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{\left(2\pi f\right)^{2}\tau^{2}+1}+\frac{\sigma}{\varepsilon_{0}}
$$
where $\tau,\varepsilon_{s},\varepsilon_{\infty},f$ and $\scriptstyle{\varepsilon_{0}}$ mean relaxation time, relative permittivity at static, high frequency limited relative dielectric permittivity, static permittivity frequency, and dielectric constant in vacuum, respectively [48,49]. S0 has higher dielectric parameters, which is attributed to the multi-polarization effect and a large number of defects. As the proportion of Hmim increases, the electromagnetic parameters gradually decrease. Based on the above discussion, it is found that the core–shell structure prepared by the ligand exchange-guided phase inversion is closely related to the dielectric response. The dielectric loss is affected by relaxation loss or polarization loss and conduction loss. On the one hand, the reduced e’’ value indicates that the conductive loss is gradually optimized with the construction of a new carbon skeleton, which can be studied according to the theory of free electrons with the following equation [50,51]:
$$
\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}=\frac{\sigma}{2\pi\varepsilon_{0}}\frac{1}{f}
$$
Along with the phase inversion, its conductivity gradually decreases, and its conductivity loss gradually decreases. On the other hand, the polarization relaxation loss is studied by the Cole-Cole semicircle [52,53].
$$
\left(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}+\left(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\right)^{2}=\left(\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}
$$
Typically, the Debye polarization relaxation includes ionic polarization, interfacial polarization, electrons polarization and dipoles polarization. The ionic polarization and electronic polarization always exist in the high-frequency band $(10^{3}~-10^{6}~\mathrm{GHz})$ [54,55]. Hence, dipoles polarization and interfacial polarization are the main mechanisms for the multiple relaxation processes. It is worth noting that if the curve of $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ versus e’’ shows a complete semicircle named Cole-Cole semicircle, each semicircle represents a Debye relaxation process [56,57]. Obviously, all samples have a dielectric relaxation process, which is caused by the core–shell structure of ${\mathsf{A g@C}}$ . More importantly, the Cole-Cole semicircles are gradually obvious, which proves that the polarization relaxation gradually becomes significant. At the same time, the S4 sample has more Cole-Cole semicircles. Combined with the previous complex permittivity, it shows that the contribution of Debye dielectric relaxation to the dielectric constant gradually increases with the increase of Hmim content, which can be ascribed to the tunable ligand exchanging phase inversion. The construction of a new carbon framework is conducive for the continuous increase of heterointerfaces and dipoles with the change of particle size. This can enhance the interface polarization and improve the impedance matching.
According to the transmission line theory, the EMW absorption performance is characterized through the RL value by the following formula [58,59]:
$$
\operatorname{RL}(\mathrm{dB})=20\log|\frac{Z_{\mathrm{in}}-Z_{0}}{Z_{\mathrm{in}}+Z_{0}}|
$$
$$
{\mathrm{Z_{in}}}={\mathrm{Z_{0}}}{\sqrt{\frac{{\upmu_{\mathrm{r}}}}{{\upvarepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}}}}}\mathrm{tanh(j}{\frac{2\pi f d}{c}}{\sqrt{\mu_{\mathrm{r}}\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}}})
$$
$$
t_{m}={\frac{n\gamma}{4}}={\frac{n c}{4f_{m}\sqrt{\left|\mu_{\varepsilon}\right|\left|\varepsilon_{r}\right|}}}(n=1,3,5,\ldots) 
$$
$t_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $f_{\mathrm{m}}$ signify the matching thickness and matching frequency, respectively [63].
The primary EMW absorption mechanism is analyzed according to the impedance matching and attenuation constant. On one hand, when the value of $\lvert Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}\rvert$ (calculated by Equation (6)) is close to 1, it would prevent disadvantageous surface reflection and facilitate interior multiple scattering, for the material to have better impedance matching. On the other hand, higher $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{a}}$ value implies a stronger EM attenuation capacity, which can be calculated based on the following equation [64,65]:
$$
\alpha=\frac{\sqrt{2}\pi f}{c}\sqrt{\left(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)+\sqrt{\left(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)^{2}+\left(\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}+\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)^{2}}}
$$
The $\lvert Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}\rvert$ values of the precursors are obviously far away from 1, as shown in Fig. S8. However, the $\lvert Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}\rvert$ values of S0, and S2 are all close to 1 compared to those of S1, S3 and S4 (Fig. 4(a1-a4) and Fig. S9). This shows that S0 and S2 have great impedance matching characteristics. This is attributed to the proper ratio of ligand exchanging phase inversion to optimize reflection loss and internal scattering loss through the core–shell structure model. In Fig. 4(b), taking representative $\alpha$ curves into consideration, S0 and S2 obtain excellent $\alpha$ values, further confirming their higher electromagnetic energy dissipation capacities. The ligand exchange-guided phase inversion contributed to improving the EMW attenuation ability of the sample; while too much Hmim destroyed the newly formed MOFs skeleton, making the $\alpha$ value of S4 to be less than that of S3. For the precursors of S2 and S0, there are also relatively good $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{a}}$ values (Fig. S10). And the decay exponential is still going up, showing the factors of absorbance performance are the synergistic effects of impedance matching and attenuation constant. Both S0 and S2 have a thinner thickness (Fig. 4(c)), indicating that they have better application prospects; while their precursors do not have a good thickness (Fig. S11). For S0, S1and S2, appropriate levels of ligand exchange-guided phase inversion made the absorbers to have a thinner thickness in 2–18 GHZ. However, superfluous Hmim caused more severe damage to the new MOFs skeleton, resulting in increased thickness; an example is S4. In Fig. 4(d), Fig. S12 and Fig. S13, the characteristics of magnetic loss and dielectric loss are evaluated by calculating the tangent of dielectric loss (tan $\begin{array}{r}{\delta_{\varepsilon}=\frac{\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}}{\varepsilon^{\prime}},}\end{array}$ and magnetic loss (tan $\begin{array}{r}{\delta_{\mu}=\frac{\mu^{\prime\prime}}{\mu^{\prime}})}\end{array}$ [66,67]. Results indicate that the ability of the samples to attenuate EMW is completely attributed to their dielectric loss. The appropriate ratio of ligand exchange-guided phase inversion enhanced the interface polarization and conduction loss of the prepared core–shell $\mathsf{A g@C}$ samples to improve their ability to attenuate EMW. In Fig. 4(e), the dielectric loss mechanism is further analyzed by EIS curves. The smaller arc radius of S0 and S2 indicates their faster charge transfer kinetics, which corresponds to higher $\scriptstyle{\varepsilon_{r}}$ values.
The EMW absorption mechanism is illustrated in Scheme 2. Firstly, the hollow core–shell products derived from the ligand exchange-guided phase inversion are loose and porous. This unique structure makes it easier for them to capture electromagnetic waves. Secondly, the dislocations and defects caused by void formation promote the formation of polarization centers. And the multi-defect in carbon shells can also generate multiple polarizations, which will consume the EMW. In addition, the dual-ligand metal–organic framework obtained by tunable ligand exchangeguided phase inversion is conducive to forming a heterogeneous interface after calcination. In detail, the heterogeneous interfaces between the carbon shells and Ag particles as well as gas–solid interfaces have the ability to dissipate EMW by generating interfacial polarization [68]. That is, opposite charges are accumulated and distributed on the heterogeneous interfaces to attenuate electromagnetic energy [69]. Moreover, the carbon shells and Ag particles were also used to construct the conductive network with migration conductance and jumping conductance, which generated a good synergistic effect and led to an increase in the conductive loss of the $\mathsf{A g@C}$ complex material [70]. Finally, this special core–shell structure will lead to multiple scattering and reflection, further dissipating EMW.
Fig. 5 and Table 1 summarize the EMW absorption properties of the representative core–shell EMAs reported in recent literature. As we can see, the as-prepared $\mathsf{A g@C}$ absorbers have better EMW absorption performances compared to other various core–shell EMAs including magnetic ferrites, semiconductors, carbon materials and conductive polymers, etc. Therefore, the well-designed core–shell $\mathsf{A g@C}$ can be defined as ideal dielectric type core–shell EMAs, and the ligand exchanging dominated phase inversion can be an effective strategy to fabricate MOFs derived EMAs.
